    "parsing_res_list": [
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "### 5.1 Recursive induction",
            "block_bbox": [
                139,
                145,
                494,
                175
            ],
            "block_id": 0,
            "block_order": 1,
            "group_id": 0,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    139.0,
                    145.0
                ],
                [
                    494.0,
                    145.0
                ],
                [
                    494.0,
                    175.0
                ],
                [
                    139.0,
                    175.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The decision tree. A tree  $ \\mathcal{T} $ partitions the rows into a set  $ \\mathcal{L}(\\mathcal{T}) $ of disjoint leaves  $ \\ell $; on each leaf a separate twoblock model  $ (\\mathbf{W}_{\\ell}, \\mathbf{B}_{\\ell}) $ is fit to its members. The twolock clusterin tree grows this tree by recursive top-down induction. At the root, a twoblock model – with the objective of choice, covariance (2), coskewness (4) or the combination (5) – is fitted on all  $ n $ rows; the first score  $ t_1 $ of that model defines a one-dimensional ordering of the rows along which a cut  $ \\tau $ is searched. Once a cut is committed, the rows split into a left child (rows with  $ t_{i1} < \\tau $) and a right child; on each child a new twoblock model is fit and the recursion continues until a stopping rule fires.",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                192,
                1084,
                421
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 1,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    136.0,
                    192.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    192.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    421.0
                ],
                [
                    136.0,
                    421.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The decomposition objective enters a node in two distinct roles, and tbtree allows them to differ. In its splitting role it shapes the first score  $ t_{1} $ along which the cut is searched: with the covariance objective  $ t_{1} $ is the direction of maximal joint  $ \\mathbf{X}-\\mathbf{Y} $ covariance and tbtree reduces essentially to a twoblock analogue of PLS-Trees (Eriksson et al., 2009); with the coskewness objective  $ t_{1} $ is instead the direction of maximal joint skewness, which is assumed to be the direction along which two regimes are most cleanly separated. In its leaf role the fitted model  $ (\\mathbf{W}_{\\ell}, \\mathbf{B}_{\\ell}) $ supplies the prediction  $ \\hat{\\mathbf{y}}_{i} = \\mathbf{x}_{i}^{\\top} \\mathbf{B}_{\\ell} $. These two roles have opposing ideal objectives: separating regimes is a third-moment property, so the split benefits from coskewness (or the combined objective), whereas predicting inside a leaf is an ordinary regression, for which a skewness-aligned direction is a poor axis – it deliberately trades away the within-regime covariance that a leaf regression needs.",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                423,
                1084,
                738
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    136.0,
                    423.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    423.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    738.0
                ],
                [
                    136.0,
                    738.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The recommended, and throughout Sections 6–7 default, configuration therefore decouples the two: the tree is grown and routed with a coskewness or combined split objective, while every leaf model  $ B_{\\ell} $ is fit under the plain covariance objective. Operationally the tree structure and its routing directions  $ w_{1} $ are fixed first, and each leaf is then refit under the covariance objective on its own members; the internal routing, computable from X alone at prediction time, keeps the skewness-aligned split. This decoupled tree retains the regime separation of the third-moment split while paying none of its predictive cost, and it is the form intended whenever a coskewness or cov+coskew tbtree is referred to in the benchmarks below. Using the coskewness objective in the leaves as well is an option, but is preferable only in niche settings where the leaf directions are themselves the object of study. For instance, reading a leaf's third-moment loadings to see which variables drive that regime's asymmetry could be a niche setting in which such a coupled variant would be preferred. Howbeit, on every predictive benchmark investigated here, it was dominated by the decoupled variant.",
            "block_bbox": [
                137,
                740,
                1084,
                1118
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    137.0,
                    740.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    740.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    1118.0
                ],
                [
                    137.0,
                    1118.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Stopping rules. A node becomes a leaf if (i) its depth reaches a user-set  $ d_{\\text{max}} $, (ii) it contains fewer than  $ 2n_{\\text{min}} $ rows, where  $ n_{\\text{min}} $ is the minimum enforced on each child, or (iii) the cross-validation gate of Section 5.3 refuses the candidate split. Otherwise the cut minimizing the chosen impurity is accepted and the recursion continues.",
            "block_bbox": [
                137,
                1149,
                1084,
                1265
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    137.0,
                    1149.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    1149.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    1265.0
                ],
                [
                    137.0,
                    1265.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Prediction. For a new row, tbtree routes it from the root downward by computing the first X-score of each node's fitted model and comparing it to that node's stored cut, until a leaf is reached; the leaf's twoblock model then gives  $ \\hat{y}_i = x_i^\\top B_\\ell $. The routing uses  $ t_1 = x_i^\\top w_1 $, which is computable from X alone at prediction time; this is why the cut is searched on",
            "block_bbox": [
